@*
    Every message body on the site renders through here.

    Before this, three surfaces each did their own thing: the org message view dropped raw HTML in
    with MarkupString, the message list ran a private regex to strip tags for its snippet, and the
    case thread printed plain text. Three renderers means three places to change when the way a
    message is written changes — and the next change is exactly that: @mentions and #hashtags need
    linkifying, once, everywhere, rather than three times with three sets of edge cases.

    So this is deliberately the only place that decides what a message body looks like.

    On sanitisation: org message bodies are authored in a rich-text editor and stored as HTML, and
    are rendered here as HTML. That is not new — it is what the message view already did — but it
    is worth naming, because this component is where a sanitiser belongs when one is added rather
    than at each call site.
*@

@if (Snippet)
{
    <text>@PlainText()</text>
}
else if (Html)
{
    @((MarkupString)Body)
}
else
{
    @* Plain text, with line breaks honoured. A case message typed across three lines should read
       as three lines rather than one run-on paragraph. *@
    <div style="white-space:pre-wrap;">@Body</div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The message body as stored.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Body { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>True when <see cref="Body"/> holds HTML rather than plain text.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Html { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Render as a one-line preview: tags removed, whitespace collapsed.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Snippet { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How many characters a snippet keeps before it is cut.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int SnippetLength { get; set; } = 200;

    /// <summary>
    /// The body as readable text, for previews.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Tag-stripping by regex, which is wrong in general and adequate here: the input is our own
    /// editor's output, the result is truncated to a line of preview text, and it is never
    /// rendered as markup — it goes into the DOM as a string. A parser would be the right tool if
    /// this answer were ever trusted for anything.
    /// </remarks>
    private string PlainText()
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Body)) return string.Empty;

        var text = Html
            ? System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Replace(Body, "<[^>]+>", " ")
            : Body;

        text = System.Net.WebUtility.HtmlDecode(text);
        text = System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Replace(text, @"\s+", " ").Trim();

        return text.Length <= SnippetLength ? text : text[..SnippetLength].TrimEnd() + "…";
    }
}
